  {
   "chapter": "The man who crashed gold",
   "say": "Mansa Musa gave gold to almost everyone he met. Officials, princes, even the poor in the streets. Merchants charged him inflated prices, and he paid without arguing. A writer named al-Umari, who lived in Cairo a few years later, recorded what happened next.",
   "pops": [
    {
     "text": "AL-UMARI",
     "label": "Historian in Cairo",
     "on": "al-Umari,"
    }
   ],
   "visuals": [
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "Cairo bazaar painting",
      "John Frederick Lewis bazaar"
     ],
     "ai": "a crowded Cairo bazaar with merchants, oil painting",
     "zoom": "right"
    },
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "Mamluk gold dinar",
      "Mamluk coin"
     ],
     "ai": "medieval Islamic gold coins",
     "zoom": "in"
    },
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "Cairo street painting 19th century",
      "Cairo street scene painting"
     ],
     "ai": "poor people in an old Cairo street receiving gold",
     "zoom": "out"
    },
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "Arabic manuscript medieval",
      "Islamic manuscript page"
     ],
     "ai": "an old Arabic manuscript with ink and pen",
     "zoom": "in"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "Gold was at a high price in Egypt until they came in that year. From that time its value fell, and it has stayed cheap. This has been the state of affairs for about twelve years.",
   "visuals": [
    {
     "type": "quote",
     "text": "Gold was at a high price in Egypt until they came in that year... from that time its value fell... This has been the state of affairs for about twelve years.",
     "by": "al-Umari, writing in Cairo in the 1330s"
    },
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "Mamluk silver dirham",
      "silver dirham coin"
     ],
     "ai": "silver medieval coins",
     "zoom": "in"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "In numbers, before his visit, one mithqal of gold, about four grams, was worth at least twenty five silver dirhams. Afterwards, it was worth twenty two or less. One man, on one trip, had changed the value of money in an entire city.",
   "pops": [
    {
     "text": "1 MITHQAL ≈ 4 GRAMS",
     "on": "mithqal"
    }
   ],
   "visuals": [
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "gold dinar Egypt",
      "Fatimid dinar"
     ],
     "ai": "a single gold coin on a dark table",
     "zoom": "in"
    },
    {
     "type": "bars",
     "title": "One mithqal of gold in Cairo, in silver dirhams",
     "bars": [
      [
       "Before 1324",
       25
      ],
      [
       "After 1324",
       22
      ]
     ],
     "note": "Source: al-Umari"
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "lines": [
      "One man.",
      "One trip.",
      "A whole city's money changed."
     ],
     "hit": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "This is one of the earliest recorded cases of a single person changing the value of money in a whole economy. When there is suddenly much more of something, each piece of it is worth less. Today economists call it inflation. Cairo learned about it from one king.",
   "visuals": [
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "gold coins pile",
      "hoard gold coins"
     ],
     "ai": "a mountain of gold coins",
     "zoom": "out"
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "lines": [
      "More gold in the city.",
      "Each coin buys less.",
      "That is inflation."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "image",
     "search": [
      "Catalan Atlas Mansa Musa",
      "Mansa Musa Catalan Atlas detail"
     ],
     "ai": "a medieval African king with gold",
     "zoom": "in",
     "focus": [
      0.5,
      0.45
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "chapter": "Broke on the way home",
   "date": "1325",
   "say": "And here is the twist. On his way home, Mansa Musa had given away so much that he ran out. According to writers in Cairo, he had to borrow money from Egyptian merchants at very high interest. The richest man on Earth, in debt.",
